  <span class="genre-pill">SHORT STORY</span>
  <h1>The Harvest</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>She did not know his name and she did not know his face, but she dreamed about him the way you dream about people you have seen only once or twice — in fragments, in impressions, in the particular sharpness that comes from not yet having filed the experience away in the place where ordinary memories go. In the dream, they were in the Hubbard dining room. The room had walls that she had not known it had in waking life — the dream room was larger, wood-paneled, with a fireplace that was not there in the real house. The man was sitting at a table. There was a plate in front of him. She could not see what was on the plate, but she knew, with the logic of dreams, that it was food, and that he was eating, and that the act of eating was the point of the scene, the thing she was meant to understand.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He looked up. His face was in shadow but she could see enough — older than she had thought, deep lines around the eyes, a mouth that was not smiling. He looked at her the way he had looked at her through the window: as if he had been expecting her, as if she were not a surprise but a confirmation.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"You came," he said.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She woke up with her heart going fast and a taste in her mouth that she could not identify, something sour and metallic, like blood or rust. Milo was asleep beside her, which was where he slept now, on the dog bed in the corner of the bedroom that she had resisted for years and now did not think about. She lay in the dark and listened to Milo breathe and tried to understand why she was afraid.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>It was not the man in the dream that frightened her. It was not the man in the window. It was the sense — the persistent, undeniable sense — that she had not been looking at something new. That she had seen this before, in some other context, some other time, in a place she could not name and a person she could not identify. The feeling was architectural, the way déjà vu is architectural — it was not the sense of having seen a thing but the sense of having been somewhere, lived in a place, occupied a room that was also this room, was also this window, was also this man.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She got up and went to the bathroom and drank water from the tap and looked at her face in the mirror. She was sixty-one. Her face was the face that sixty-one looked like. She had lines and she had spots and she had the particular looseness of skin that came from years of sleeping on the same side. She looked at the face and she did not see anyone she recognized, and this was not new — she had been feeling this way for months — but in the bathroom at three in the morning, under the harsh light, it felt like something she was only now understanding.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She went to the Hubbard place on a Tuesday afternoon, which was the day the garbage was collected, which meant the street had people on it — the garbage truck and the men who rode on it and the neighbors who had their cans out — and she told herself she was just walking Milo, which was true, Milo needed to walk, it was healthy for both of them, and she was just taking a slightly different route, a route that happened to go past the Hubbard place, and if the route happened to be slightly longer than usual, this was not suspicious.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She did not look at the window. She looked at the lawn — still shaggy, she noted, still uncut — and she looked at the porch — still deteriorating, the steps going soft at the corners — and she looked at the front door, the door that rattled, and she did not look at the window because she had made a decision, in the three days since she had last seen the man, that she would not look at the window. She would walk past. She would be normal. She would be the kind of person who walks a dog in the late afternoon and who does not look at windows and who does not dream about men she has never met.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Milo stopped to sniff something in the grass and she stood on the sidewalk and waited for him, which gave her the excuse she needed to be standing in front of the Hubbard place for a slightly extended period of time, and during this time she did not look at the window. But she heard something. A sound from inside the house — not a voice, not footsteps, but a sound, a low and steady sound that she could not identify, that she could not place, that she knew immediately was the kind of sound that should not be coming from an empty house.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She pulled Milo away from the smell and she walked home. She did not run, because running would have meant something, would have indicated something, but she walked fast, and when she got inside she locked the door again and she did not unlock it again that night.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The man was in her kitchen when she came home from the grocery store on Saturday.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>She knew, before she saw him, that he would be there. She knew it the way you know things in the body before you know them in the mind — a tightening, a quickening, a shift in the air pressure of the house that told her she was not alone. She had not seen him come in. She had not heard him come in. She had been gone for forty minutes, to the grocery store, to the ordinary errand that was the most ordinary thing she did, and when she came back he was sitting at her kitchen table, in the chair she sat in every morning, in the light from the window that came in over the sink.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He looked older than she had thought. The lines around his eyes were deep, carved in a way that suggested years, decades, a face that had been weathered past the point of repair. He was wearing clothes she did not recognize — dark, heavy, the kind of clothes that were not fashionable and not unfashionable but simply old, the kind of clothes that did not have a decade they belonged to.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>He was looking at a photograph on her table. The photograph was one of the ones she kept in the sideboard drawer, the ones she had been putting away for months, the ones she had decided not to look at anymore because looking at them was a kind of pain she did not need. A photograph of David, from before. David at thirty, David in the backyard, David smiling in a way that he had not smiled in the last years of their marriage.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"Thomas," the man said. He did not look up.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"What?"</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"His name was Thomas. Your husband. David Thomas Brennan." He touched the photograph with one finger, a gesture that was almost tender. "I knew him when he was young. Younger than this. He had a different face, then. A different life."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Vera set the grocery bag on the counter. She did not scream. She did not run. She stood with the bag in her hand and she felt the fear in her body, felt it moving through her the way fear does when it is past the point of being useful, and she said: "Who are you?"</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The man looked up. His eyes were dark and deep, and she saw in them the thing she had been sensing since the first night — the weight of something, the density of years, the accumulated mass of a life that was not like other lives.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"My name is Joseph Hubbard," he said. "I lived in that house. I lived there for a long time. And I need to tell you something about Thomas Brennan, and about why he left."</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
